WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
You may have seen this purple box.
SmileDirectClub.&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.120
Straighter teeth, for half the&nbsp;
price, without seeing a specialist.&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.280
It was everywhere. In YouTube&nbsp;
ads, sponsorships, the news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.040
with one of the most aggressive&nbsp;
advertising funnels, ever.&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.000
So, how did they go from this
To this?

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.920
And even stranger… still charging customers&nbsp;
for a service… that no longer exists&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.920
What happened?
It turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.280
this is one of the strangest companies ever.
Lawsuits with the entire dental industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.240
US states, and… paying to silence&nbsp;
critics and even their own customers…

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.920
I think a pain everyone can relate to is&nbsp;
dental, especially re-aligning teeth and braces&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.760
It’s extremely costly, but&nbsp;
what if it didn’t have to be?&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.960
This was the pitch of Jordan Katzman and&nbsp;
Alex Fenkell, founders of SmileDirectClub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.760
Which I'll often shorten to “SDC."
A single dental visit can quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.280
become months of travel, repeated&nbsp;
appointments with an orthodontist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.440
X-rays, and thousands of dollars out of pocket.
But with SDC, you can access dental care from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.400
the comfort of your home with a&nbsp;
$79 kit and an $80 monthly fee.&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.800
That’s… pretty radical. How did they do this?
Invisalign charged orthodontists $1,000 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.400
$1,500 per case for the aligners. And once&nbsp;
the orthodontist added consultation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.680
treatment fees, patients could end up paying&nbsp;
$4,000 to $5,000 for the full treatment.&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.280
So, SDC cut out the orthodontist and brought the&nbsp;
aligners directly to the customer. Their pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:45.200
was 50-60% less than traditional treatment.
To get in, they mail you a DIY dental kit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.920
that you simply bite into and mail back, or you&nbsp;
could simply walk into a SmileShop for a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:57.440
3D intraoral scan. These shops were everywhere,&nbsp;
growing from 121 shops in 34 states to over 300.&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.800
The scan result or mold is reviewed remotely by a&nbsp;
licensed dentist or orthodontist who will create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.400
treatment plan. Once approved, the full set of&nbsp;
aligners will be produced and shipped to you.&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.640
To sign up, you don’t need a credit check,&nbsp;
making this easier for those who can’t afford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.120
traditional dental care to get treatment.
We're not trying to replace orthodontists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.320
We're trying to reach the 85% of&nbsp;
people who need treatment but can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.840
afford it or won't go through the hassle.
Fast and easy, right? Well, both of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.120
aren't always best, especially in healthcare.
They also had to convince a lot of people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.200
trust a website… and a stranger they’d&nbsp;
never meet, with their dental health.&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.280
So, SDC operated one of the most aggressive&nbsp;
customer-acquisition funnels in DTC history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.960
And boy, were they spending a lot.
For example, in 2019, SDC spent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.880
staggering $481.5 million on sales and&nbsp;
marketing to generate roughly 453,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.880
unique aligner orders. That's an estimated&nbsp;
$1,060 to acquire each converted customer.&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.280
This would cause some problems down the&nbsp;
line. But for now, they were growing fast.&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:11.440
SDC went from making just $20.6 million in revenue&nbsp;
in 2016 to making $750 million by 2019. And within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.280
that same stretch, they grew to 6,300 employees.
Even Align Technology, the company behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:23.920
Invisalign, took a 19% stake in SDC.
By 2017, SDC was already valued at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.600
roughly $640 million.
With this success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.920
SDC went public on September 12th, 2019.
The company raised $1.35 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.760
and on paper, it looked like a massive success.
Its founders, Jordan Katzman and Alex Fenkell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.680
became billionaires almost overnight, with the&nbsp;
company reaching an $8.9 billion valuation.&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.360
But there was a problem.
SDC initially priced its shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:56.000
at $19 to $22, but strong investor demand pushed&nbsp;
the company and its bankers to settle on $23 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.240
share. The higher price meant more cash for SDC.
Then investors got their first look at what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.440
were actually buying, sending the stock&nbsp;
down 27.5% on its first day and closing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.760
$16.67. This was the worst first-day performance&nbsp;
for an American IPO that raised more than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.800
billion dollars since the financial crisis.
And within five weeks, the founders were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.800
no longer billionaires… but they&nbsp;
were definitely multi-millionaires.&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.120
Four years later, SDC would file for Chapter 11&nbsp;
bankruptcy, carrying roughly $900 million in debt.&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.920
And eventually, the company would be liquidated.
So what went wrong?&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.280
Well, SDC had a lot of opposition ahead&nbsp;
of them. And some of it, very warranted.&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.120
Not only that, the direct-to-consumer brand got&nbsp;
caught up in some… rather strange controversies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:54.640
that would follow them forever.
As we'll soon learn, Smile Direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.440
Club spent an absurd amount acquiring customers.
And for every business, one of the biggest yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:05.040
least spoken about challenges is "go-to-market".
Aka, launching and reaching the right buyers.&nbsp;

