00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.840
Let's take a step back to what it actually&nbsp;
cost SDC to acquire those customers.&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.960
Because there were three big problems.
On paper, SDC's revenue was increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.080
by over 190% in just one year.
But on closer look, something was eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.840
into the company's gross profit.
So, what was it?&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.000
In 2017, the company pulled&nbsp;
in $146 million in revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.880
but spent $64.2 million on sales and marketing&nbsp;
alone. That's roughly 44% of everything it made.&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.400
Revenue jumped to $423.2 million in 2018,&nbsp;
but sales and marketing spending exploded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:50.160
to $213.1 million, eating up more than&nbsp;
50% of the company's total revenue.&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.320
But it gets worse.
Unlike SaaS, or even other direct-to-consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.280
subscription businesses like shaving clubs or&nbsp;
contact-lens companies, clear aligners aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.720
something customers keep buying every month.
So SDC had to keep spending heavily to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.760
find a new wave of customers every year.
Remember those monthly payments? Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:16.000
paying the full cost upfront, customers could pay&nbsp;
just $250 and spread the rest over two years. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.120
about 60% of customers chose that option.
But that came with a price.&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:28.640
By the end of 2021, customers owed SDC about&nbsp;
$243.8 million. And that year, missed payments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.560
added up to about 9% of SDC's revenue.
By September 2022, that had dropped to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:41.920
about $202 million. And by the end of 2022, missed&nbsp;
payments had climbed to 11% of revenue. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:47.440
more and more customers were falling behind
Things like expected discounts, cancellations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.640
refunds, and customers failing to pay could all&nbsp;
reduce the amount the company expected to collect.&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.400
For example, in the first nine months of 2022,&nbsp;
SDC recorded about $73.2 million in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:05.280
reductions against $384.2 million in revenue.&nbsp;
That's roughly 19% of the revenue figure.&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.600
SDC claimed that only 1% of its&nbsp;
customers complained after treatment.&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.960
But 1% of 1 million is still 10,000 people.
And some of those customers were never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.480
told to see a dentist at all.
A hidden-camera investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:24.320
by NBC Nightly News exposed SmileDirectClub.&nbsp;
Employees at SmileShops in Ohio, New Jersey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.960
and Alabama told prospective customers they&nbsp;
didn’t need to see a dentist beforehand.&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:32.880
And the damage that not seeing a&nbsp;
dentist could cause goes even deeper.&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.800
Dr. Chung Kau, chair of orthodontics at UAB,&nbsp;
told NBC that moving teeth without in-person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.600
supervision could cause permanent harm.
And the FDA's own database contains some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.560
disturbing accounts from&nbsp;
SmileDirectClub customers.&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.960
"After a visit with the orthodontic and&nbsp;
periodontic dept at the univ of (b)(6) it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.120
was determined that due to my smile direct&nbsp;
club treatment, that the aligners pushed my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.600
bottom teeth out of the bone causing them to&nbsp;
be mobile and causes severe gum recession on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.880
all front teeth top and bottom."
Ouch. So, how did SDC respond?&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:10.080
"That may be a Smile guide who didn't actually&nbsp;
have or remember the proper training."&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.960
That’s not all.
On May 18, 2020, SDC sued NBCUniversal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:20.000
and reporter Vicky Nguyen for approximately&nbsp;
$2.8 billion, accusing them of making more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.560
than 40 false and misleading statements.
Their main concern was that the report had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.640
wiped $950 million off its market value.
But NBC did not back down. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:37.040
this lawsuit was dismissed in December 2021.
But it gets way, WAY worse.&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.440
CBC found that some customers couldn't even&nbsp;
complain because they'd signed agreements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.280
that prevented them from talking&nbsp;
publicly about their experience.&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.640
And this eventually became the&nbsp;
subject of a lawsuit from Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.520
D.C.'s Attorney General, who revealed that&nbsp;
customers who wanted refunds after 30 days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.640
were sometimes required to sign an NDA.
And to sign said NDA, they had to delete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:03.520
negative reviews they'd already posted.
They could also face $10,000 penalties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.880
for each violation.
And it didn't stop there.&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:12.160
The agreements allegedly prevented customers from&nbsp;
even filing complaints with government regulators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.120
including when those complaints&nbsp;
involved people who had suffered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:22.160
“painful and sometimes permanent injuries.”
Then, on June 22, 2023, SDC settled the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:27.680
paying $500,000 and releasing more than&nbsp;
17,000 customers from the gag provisions.&nbsp;

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:31.440
But SDC still pushed back.
"For too long there has been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.240
misinformation campaign claiming SmileDirectClub&nbsp;
stifles negative consumer feedback through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:40.480
use of non-disclosure agreements."
But, there was another big problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.960
And you might’ve thought about it during&nbsp;
this video: What about the dental industry?&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.680
You know, all the thousands of&nbsp;
orthodontists, practices, associations…&nbsp;

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.480
There’s no way they would’ve rolled over&nbsp;
and let SmileDirectClub dominate… right?&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.200
And you’re right.
Which brings us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:01.840
SmileDirectClub’s biggest battle yet.
Which, ironically, would persist beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:11.520
the company’s own lifespan...
If you’ve watched this far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.920
consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.800
There is a reason why people are meant to visit an&nbsp;
orthodontist with years of postgraduate training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.000
Or take X-rays, physical exams, follow-ups.
Even for the small-to-moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:31.040
cases which SDC operated in.
But SDC took a complex medical service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.680
and turned much of it into a product.
And many were not happy about this.&nbsp;

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.600
While SDC reported growth into multiple&nbsp;
states, patients started showing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.920
in orthodontists' offices with SDC&nbsp;
treatment that needed to be fixed.&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:50.560
So the American Association of Orthodontists, or&nbsp;
AAO, launched a campaign against SmileDirectClub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:54.080
This included filing complaints with&nbsp;
more than 35 state dental boards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:58.320
authorities, alleging that the company's&nbsp;
practices could put the public at risk.&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.720
In Alaska, the dental board unanimously&nbsp;
sent SDC a cease-and-desist.&nbsp;

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:08.400
In response, SDC prepared its lawyers.
In October 2017, it sent its own cease-and-desist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.560
letters to dentists over YouTube reviews,&nbsp;
sued orthodontists in New York and New Jersey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:17.040
and even sued the Michigan Dental Association&nbsp;
over four paragraphs it had published.&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.960
And this was all before SDC even went public.
Then the states started pushing back.&nbsp;

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:28.080
In 2018, Georgia passed a rule requiring dental&nbsp;
assistants taking orthodontic scans to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:33.520
licensed dentist immediately supervising them,&nbsp;
effectively blocking SDC's SmileShop model.&nbsp;

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:39.360
So SDC sued Georgia, arguing that taking&nbsp;
digital scans wasn't the practice of dentistry.&nbsp;

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.240
But Georgia wasn't the only&nbsp;
state SDC was fighting.&nbsp;

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.400
The company filed similar lawsuits in Alabama, and&nbsp;
later California. (show, highlight “California”)&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:51.280
It even took the fight across the border,&nbsp;
going after dental associations in Manitoba.&nbsp;

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:56.000
Then the fight reached the federal level.
The American Dental Association petitioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:00.000
the FDA, arguing that SmileDirectClub&nbsp;
was providing prescription-only medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.160
devices without requiring customers&nbsp;
to undergo an in-person examination.&nbsp;

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:08.320
The ADA and AAO later took&nbsp;
their complaints to the FTC.&nbsp;

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:12.400
And in May 2019, a federal judge in&nbsp;
Georgia ruled that taking digital scans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:17.440
for treatment planning “falls squarely within&nbsp;
the definition of the practice of dentistry.”&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:21.280
Yet SDC was going public while&nbsp;
all of this was happening.&nbsp;

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:24.960
Then, on January 6, 2020,&nbsp;
nine members of Congress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:29.680
five of them dentists, asked the FDA&nbsp;
and FTC to investigate SmileDirectClub.&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.880
With lawsuits, regulators, and Congress&nbsp;
closing in, how long could SDC hold up?&nbsp;

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.880
With all these legal fights, and&nbsp;
rapid expansion, are pretty expensive.&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:46.720
But SmilePay wasn't bringing in cash fast enough.
So, in 2022, SDC borrowed up to $255 million from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:52.160
a private lender called HPS. And to get it,&nbsp;
they used their SmilePay payments as security.&nbsp;

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:58.080
Over 60% of customers were paying through&nbsp;
SmilePay, which is SDC's monthly payment plan.&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.560
Basically, SDC borrowed against money&nbsp;
their customers still owed them.&nbsp;

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:06.800
But, it meant SDC was waiting to&nbsp;
collect much of its revenue over time.&nbsp;

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:10.240
Then inflation and interest&nbsp;
rates went through the roof.&nbsp;

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:15.360
Customers started falling behind on payments,&nbsp;
making those receivables harder to collect.&nbsp;

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:21.200
By late 2023, SDC was carrying around&nbsp;
$891 million in debt liabilities.&nbsp;

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.280
But somehow, it gets much worse.
In 2023, an arbitrator ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:30.240
SDC to pay Align Technology roughly&nbsp;
$63 million over a patent dispute.&nbsp;

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:36.240
Then, on September 29, SDC filed for Chapter&nbsp;
11 bankruptcy to restructure and find a buyer.&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.720
But they found none.
Just over two months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:44.960
later, SDC abruptly shut down.
But… the story, unfortunately, didn’t end there.&nbsp;

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:50.640
Thousands of customers were left with unfinished&nbsp;
treatments, but their bills didn't disappear.&nbsp;

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:55.920
SDC's shutdown FAQ told SmilePay customers they&nbsp;
were still expected to make their monthly payments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:59.280
until the balance was paid in full.
Yeah.&nbsp;

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:04.480
And regulators eventually stepped in.
In late 2024, the New York Attorney General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:10.480
finalised a $4.8 million restitution agreement&nbsp;
covering more than 28,000 consumers nationwide.&nbsp;

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:15.200
But even then, many customers who had already&nbsp;
received their aligners remained obligated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:18.960
pay their financing contracts.
SmileDirectClub, was a strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.320
direct-to-consumer experiment.
Innovative and disruptive…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:28.480
but maybe not in a good way.
During the lawsuits, 77% of orthodontists surveyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.880
by the AAO said they had seen new patients&nbsp;
needing retreatment after using mail-order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:38.480
aligners without an in-person exam. I think&nbsp;
that's the irony at the heart of SmileDirectClub.&nbsp;

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:45.360
The low price came from removing the dentist.
That put SDC at war with an entire profession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.080
They won some battles in court, but lost the&nbsp;
cash to keep going because their product was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:53.920
essentially a one-time purchase.
And even then, I wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:59.120
call SmileDirectClub the good guys.
SmileDirectClub is one of many gigantic brands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:04.080
that used the “direct-to-consumer” model. A&nbsp;
lot of these started exploding in the 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:09.520
and one of the biggest, was Dollar Shave Club.
A box of razors every month that single-handedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:13.600
topped the razor industry… only&nbsp;
to collapse several years later.&nbsp;

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:17.280
So, why do direct-to-consumer&nbsp;
brands keep collapsing?&nbsp;

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:20.080
Watch this video next to&nbsp;
learn the rest of the story.

